      "navTitle": "Getting Started",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-29",
      "slug": "readme",
      "file": "getting-started.md",
      "section": "Guides",
      "path": "/projects/webview-native-ui-engine/docs/readme",
      "html": "<p data-line=\"0\" class=\"code-line\"><strong>Bring Web UI above the native layer.</strong></p>\n<p data-line=\"2\" class=\"code-line\"><em>Visually, through seamless native-to-Web projection.</em></p>\n<p data-line=\"4\" class=\"code-line\"><img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/webview-native-ui/engine/v0.0.8/docs/assets/hero.png\" alt=\"Conceptual view of a Web overlay above a native surface and WebView content\" class=\"md-img\" loading=\"eager\" fetchpriority=\"high\"></p>\n<p data-line=\"6\" class=\"code-line\"><em>Conceptual view. Native appearance is projected into the Web layer during overlay presentation.</em></p>\n<p data-line=\"8\" class=\"code-line\">Build WebView apps where native controls and Web content share the same scrolling screens, transitions and overlays.</p>\n<h2 id=\"beyond-the-webview-boundary\" data-line=\"10\" class=\"code-line\"><a class=\"header-anchor-link\" href=\"#beyond-the-webview-boundary\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></a> Beyond the WebView boundary</h2>\n<p data-line=\"12\" class=\"code-line\">WebView apps combine the Web's flexible layouts with access to native platform capabilities. But native UI and the Web page live in separate rendering layers. A native control placed above the WebView does not automatically participate in DOM scrolling, clipping or stacking order. A Web modal cannot simply cover it with a higher <code>z-index</code>.</p>\n<p data-line=\"14\" class=\"code-line\">Crossing that boundary takes more than a bridge call. It requires coordinating placement, appearance, input, rendering and restoration across two rendering systems on each platform. Rebuilding that coordination for every component makes otherwise useful designs expensive to deliver and maintain.</p>\n<p data-line=\"16\" class=\"code-line\">WebView Native UI Engine makes that work reusable. It lets applications combine native UI with Web-driven screens and interactions, while sharing the machinery that moves presentation between the two layers. The goal is to expand what a WebView app can be built to do—and make that practical to ship.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-this-makes-possible\" data-line=\"18\" class=\"code-line\"><a class=\"header-anchor-link\" href=\"#what-this-makes-possible\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></a> What this makes possible</h2>\n<ul data-line=\"20\" class=\"code-line\">\n<li data-line=\"20\" class=\"code-line\"><strong>Native UI within Web-designed screens.</strong> Combine native toolbars, tab bars, player controls and eligible SDK views with the application's DOM layout and navigation.</li>\n<li data-line=\"21\" class=\"code-line\"><strong>Web overlays that coexist with native content.</strong> Present modals and other overlays over native surfaces, then return to the interactive native UI when they close.</li>\n<li data-line=\"22\" class=\"code-line\"><strong>Scrolling and transitions that retain native appearance.</strong> Carry the native surface's captured appearance through Web-layer movement, without recreating its styling in CSS.</li>\n<li data-line=\"23\" class=\"code-line\"><strong>Shared infrastructure across components and platforms.</strong> Reuse the same projection and restoration lifecycle on iOS and Android instead of implementing it independently in every integration.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-approach\" data-line=\"25\" class=\"code-line\"><a class=\"header-anchor-link\" href=\"#the-approach\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></a> The approach</h2>\n<p data-line=\"27\" class=\"code-line\">Use the live native view where it belongs, and a prepared visual projection in the DOM when the Web layer needs to own presentation. Restore the interactive native view when it can take over again. An existing DOM container gives the Web projection its place in the application's layout and layer structure.</p>\n<p data-line=\"29\" class=\"code-line\">The Engine coordinates this handoff; the integrating renderer still creates and positions its views. A projected image preserves appearance, not live interaction or video. Capture availability and layout support determine when a projection can be used, with an explicit fallback when it cannot. See <a href=\"/projects/webview-native-ui-engine/docs/concepts\">concepts and limits</a> for the detailed contract and limits.</p>\n<h2 id=\"choose-your-starting-point\" data-line=\"31\" class=\"code-line\"><a class=\"header-anchor-link\" href=\"#choose-your-starting-point\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></a> Choose your starting point</h2>\n<p data-line=\"33\" class=\"code-line\">Applications gain these capabilities through plugins that integrate the Engine.<br>\nPlugin authors retain their existing native controls and public APIs; the Engine<br>\nsupplies the coordination between native and Web presentation. It supports iOS<br>\nand Android with Capacitor or Cordova. Ionic and Angular are not required.</p>\n<table data-line=\"38\" class=\"code-line\">\n<thead data-line=\"38\" class=\"code-line\">\n<tr data-line=\"38\" class=\"code-line\">\n<th>Start here</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody data-line=\"40\" class=\"code-line\">\n<tr data-line=\"40\" class=\"code-line\">\n<td><a href=\"/projects/webview-native-ui-engine/docs/overview\">Documentation</a></td>\n<td>Reading order: basic initialization, optional behavior, advanced APIs and reference.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-line=\"41\" class=\"code-line\">\n<td><a href=\"/projects/webview-native-ui-engine/docs/concepts\">Concepts and limits</a></td>\n<td>Decide whether projection fits your component.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-line=\"42\" class=\"code-line\">\n<td><a href=\"/projects/webview-native-ui-engine/docs/getting-started/initialization\">Basic initialization</a></td>\n<td>Minimum setup and required inputs, before framework-specific integration.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-line=\"44\" class=\"code-line\">Native registration starts the Web runtime; the base integration needs no separate<br>\napplication-side Engine initializer. The API is pre-1.0: pin matching Engine<br>\nversions across npm and native dependencies. Android placement requires API 23+,<br>\nwith composited snapshots available from API 26+.</p>\n<h2 id=\"reference-and-contributing\" data-line=\"49\" class=\"code-line\"><a class=\"header-anchor-link\" href=\"#reference-and-contributing\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></a> Reference and contributing</h2>\n<p data-line=\"51\" class=\"code-line\">The <a href=\"/projects/webview-native-ui-engine/docs/overview\">documentation index</a> links to configuration, advanced APIs,<br>\ncontainer support and platform contracts. For repository development and publishing,<br>\nsee <a href=\"/projects/webview-native-ui-engine/docs/maintenance\">development and releases</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"license\" data-line=\"55\" class=\"code-line\"><a class=\"header-anchor-link\" href=\"#license\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></a> License</h2>\n<p data-line=\"57\" class=\"code-line\"><a href=\"https://github.com/webview-native-ui/engine/blob/v0.0.8/LICENSE\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\">MIT</a>. Commercial use, modification and redistribution are permitted with the license's notice and attribution requirements. The Engine's license does not change the licenses of applications or plugins that depend on it.</p>\n",
